- op: discuss  # 6c53
  at: '2026-05-10T06:52:33Z'  # 6c53
  by: agent  # 6c53
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6c53
  clock: 6  # 6c53
  nonce: 6c53  # 6c53
  message: "Stripping awaits_bakeoff_validation + for_uat_bakeoff per Bucket-E (misapplied) reclassification during hg-uat-v5.0.1 campaign scoping. Rationale:\n\n(1) Origin is structural, not empirical. Per the item's own description: filed during the cross-language framework-dispatch linker survey 2026-05-07 as a structural-fix-scope-expansion of WI-halat — 'expected real-world incidence is lower than the broader item's targets ... but the gap exists structurally.' No real-world bug observation triggered the filing.\n\n(2) Acceptance criteria are property-test / coverage only. The PR (#197) acceptance section names: property test for the two-level chain, direct-embedding regression guard, and 100% coverage on the changed source. There is no quantitative real-world claim to ground-truth.\n\n(3) The bakeoff-validation tag was attached because the BVT auto-tag rule fires when the discussion mentions a future bakeoff target ('a Go gRPC ttrpc / CSI codebase like containerd or kata-containers protects this verdict'), but that mention is speculative — not a quantitative metric. This is a misapplication of the tagging discipline.\n\n(4) Empirical search for a real-world target failed. v4.1.0 round-07 verdicted inconclusive on containerd because containerd uses only direct (1-hop) embedding. During hg-uat-v5.0.1 scoping I surveyed the locally-available Go corpus for multi-hop UnimplementedXxxServer/Service embedding chains (kata-containers, otel-collector, vault, containerd) — zero hits across all four. The pattern is rare enough in real-world Go grpc/ttrpc code that no convenient validation target exists.\n\n(5) The structural fix is preserved. _transitive_bases.collect_transitive_base_names is wired through grpc.py's existing_edges path; property tests guard the mechanism; direct-embedding regression-guard tests guard against breaking the common case. A future UAT round can re-tag this item if a real-world Go target with multi-hop embedding chains surfaces.\n\nThis strip is recorded as a Phase-2 anomaly clean-up per process-validation-queue-with-bakeoffs-and-uat.md before the hg-uat-v5.0.1 campaign begins. Worth noting as a queue-anomaly find: the BVT auto-tagging rule treats 'discussion mentions a future bakeoff' the same as 'PR claims a quantitative metric.' Those are not the same thing — follow-up to the BVT discipline playbook may be warranted."  # 6c53
